.\" Man page for wembley, the GLM-4.5-Air fraggle that drives wideband-sdr.
.\" View with: man ./models/wembley/wembley.1
.TH WEMBLEY 1 "2026-10-11" "wideband-sdr" "Fraggle Rock Field Manual"
.SH NAME
wembley \- GLM-4.5-Air with a 32k context, the AI operator for wideband-sdr
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ollama create wembley \-f models/wembley/Modelfile
.PP
.B ollama run wembley
.PP
.B OLLAMA_MODEL=wembley:latest
in
.I .env
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wembley
is a named copy of
.B MichelRosselli/GLM-4.5-Air:Q4_K_M
with one change: its context window is capped at 32,768 tokens instead of the
model's full 131,072.
.PP
The smaller window keeps the whole model in the iGPU's share of system memory
(91 GB, 100% GPU) instead of spilling onto the CPU (148 GB, 32%/68% CPU/GPU).
The weights on disk are shared with the original model; wembley adds only a
small settings layer.
.PP
Wembley is the Fraggle who can never make up his mind, and who also works as
the siren for the Fraggle Rock Volunteer Fire Department. The name fits:
before the parallel tool-call fix, GLM kept asking for the same tools over and
over until it ran out of rounds. The original indecision was the app's fault,
not the model's; see
.BR HISTORY .
.SH PARAMETERS
.TP
.B num_ctx 32768
Context window. Big enough for the wideband-sdr system prompt, band plan, tool
definitions, 40 chat messages and tool results (roughly 5\(en10k tokens).
.TP
.B stop """<|system|>"" ""<|user|>"" ""<|assistant|>"""
Inherited from the base model.
.TP
.B capabilities
completion, tools, thinking. Thinking is on by default; wideband-sdr shows it
as
.I thinking\&...
in the chat bubble.
.SH USAGE WITH WIDEBAND-SDR
The stack, bottom to top:
.PP
.nf
.RS
NESDR Mini (RTL2832U + R820T)  --USB-->
rtl_tcp :1234                  --TCP-->
wideband-sdr backend :8000     <--tool calls-->  Ollama :11434 (wembley)
browser http://localhost:8000
.RE
.fi
.PP
Start it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
rtl_tcp -a 127.0.0.1 -p 1234      # terminal 1, leave running
# terminal 2: start the app as in README.md (Docker or uvicorn)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Without Docker, the backend reads the process environment, not
.IR .env ,
so load it first:
.PP
.nf
.RS
set \-a; source .env; set +a
cd backend && uvicorn main:app \-\-host 127.0.0.1 \-\-port 8000
.RE
.fi
.PP
Then pick
.B wembley
in the assistant's model dropdown once; the browser remembers it.
.PP
Wembley works best with a narrow range and a clear task, like a lab assistant
testing a hypothesis rather than a tour guide:
.PP
.RS
.I Scan 162.4 to 162.55 MHz and tune to the strongest NOAA weather station in NBFM.
.br
.I Tune to 931 MHz and characterize the strongest signal.
.br
.I Scan 118 to 137 MHz with fixed gain 35 and list any signals.
.RE
.SH PERFORMANCE
Measured 2026-10-11 on an AMD Hawk Point laptop (iGPU, 124 GB RAM, 96 GiB GTT),
with the wideband-sdr system prompt and tools (1,858 prompt tokens):
.PP
.TS
l r r.
	original (131k)	wembley (32k)
memory	148 GB split	91 GB 100% GPU
prompt reading	18.9 tok/s	20.5 tok/s
reply writing	3.2 tok/s	4.0 tok/s
.TE
.PP
The speed gain is modest because the iGPU and CPU share the same RAM, and
generation is limited mainly by memory bandwidth. The main win is the 57 GB of
RAM handed back.
.PP
Expect a cold load (from disk) of about 5\(en6 minutes, a warm load (file
still cached) of about 40 seconds, and about 90 seconds to read the system
prompt on the first round of a chat. Later rounds reuse the cached prompt
prefix and start faster.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Set in
.IR .env :
.TP
.B OLLAMA_MODEL=wembley:latest
Default model for the assistant.
.TP
.B OLLAMA_TIMEOUT=600
Seconds per Ollama request. Raised from 180 for slow loads and long thinking.
.TP
.B MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS=10
Model replies per chat turn before giving up. Each reply may call several
tools.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I models/wembley/Modelfile
Recipe. Edit the number and rebuild with
.BR "ollama create wembley -f" " on this file."
.TP
.I models/wembley/wembley.1
This page.
.TP
.I .env
App settings; copy from
.IR .env.example .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B ollama ps shows the model stuck on Stopping...
Something still has a request open, usually the web app server finishing a
chat. Stop the server; the first Ctrl-C waits for requests, a second one quits
at once. The model unloads within seconds.
.TP
.B UI keeps connecting and disconnecting every ~5 s
rtl_tcp is holding a dongle that went away (laptop slept, or the stick was
unplugged). Ctrl-C rtl_tcp and start it again; the app reconnects on its own.
.TP
.B usb_claim_interface error -6
The kernel TV driver grabbed the dongle. Check that
.I /etc/modprobe.d/blacklist-rtlsdr.conf
blacklists
.BR dvb_usb_rtl28xxu ,
then run
.BR "sudo modprobe -r dvb_usb_rtl28xxu rtl2832" .
.TP
.B Model dropdown empty, status disconnected
Wrong Ollama host or port in the assistant panel. The port is 11434.
.TP
.B "I reached my tool-calling limit"
Should be rare since the parallel tool-call fix. If it keeps happening, raise
.B MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS
or give a narrower request.
.SH HISTORY
.TP
2026-10-11
Created. Same day, four bugs in wideband-sdr were found and fixed, each on its
own branch for a pull request to kasp0r/wideband-sdr:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B fix/parallel-tool-calls
\(em the app read the tool-call index from the wrong place, so when GLM asked
for several tools at once only the last one ran. GLM kept re-asking, which
looked like indecision. Not Wembley's fault after all.
.IP \(bu 2
.B fix/manual-tune-wins
\(em typed frequencies got overwritten, and AI scans overrode manual tuning.
.IP \(bu 2
.B fix/chat-panel-usability
\(em the chat could not be scrolled while a reply streamed.
.IP \(bu 2
.B fix/cancel-chat-on-disconnect
\(em closing the tab now stops the chat instead of leaving GLM working for an
empty room.
.RE
.SH BUGS
A chat abandoned while the model is still loading or reading the prompt is not
noticed until the first output arrives.
.PP
Cannot hear 40 m. That is the R820T tuner's floor (about 24 MHz), not
Wembley; it needs an upconverter.
